Specify all matches and verify a linear scan (@computing-linear-search: 0, is #chapter, #hephaestus-core "Derive-duplicate-aware-search-from-two-interval-boundaries.lingua#r_0F2662B6D0A811736A5817E167") { r_005C1A828D74737220FF261B83
A lookup question needs a result contract. Does the caller want a Boolean, one arbitrary match, the first match, a count, or every match? Our question is: return every row whose key equals the query, retaining the original order of those rows and every occurrence. The result is a tuple. A valid absent key returns the empty tuple; it is not an exceptional failure. An invalid query type raises TypeError and an empty string raises ValueError, even when the collection is empty.

The linear reference visits each normalized row in input order. It appends a row precisely when its key equals the query. After visiting the first j rows, the accumulated output contains exactly the matching occurrences in that prefix, in their original order. Initially both prefix and output are empty. A nonmatch leaves that property unchanged; a match appends the next required occurrence. When j reaches n, the prefix is the whole input and the invariant yields the promised result. Finite traversal establishes termination. The procedure need not know whether the input is ordered.

This is also a specification against which a different representation can be tested. For input `[("b", 20), ("a", 10), ("b", 21), ("c", 30)]`, querying b returns `(("b", 20), ("b", 21))`; querying z returns `()`. Reversing the input reverses the order of the b matches. Inserting an unmatched row changes positions but does not change the matching rows. Appending a matching row appends exactly one result occurrence. These are derived relations useful for checking, not substitutes for the invariant proof.

Normalization validates row shape and allowed types once, as described in [Trace mutation and sharing across a collection boundary](Trace-mutation-and-sharing-across-a-collection-boundary.lingua#r_0E243F4674D6A5A66298639599). The study's `scan_rows` expects the normalized tuple and validates only the query. It does not claim to be an arbitrary-data parser. Passing unsanitized objects is outside this helper's precondition. For a service accepting external data, the normalization boundary must actually be called; documenting a precondition does not enforce it at an external interface.

Counting one key equality comparison per visited row gives exactly n comparisons for this implementation, including an absent result. Returning all matches requires finishing the scan; an early exit after one match violates our contract. Result construction also requires space and work proportional to the number k of returned rows, apart from fixed overhead. Text comparison costs depend on key lengths and are refined in [Account for construction queries and returned data together](Account-for-construction-queries-and-returned-data-together.lingua#r_029F87EE3AF507AEAD87F5CC8D).

**Work and reading:** use [Introduction to Scientific Programming with Python — Sundnes](../../inquiry/reproducible-computation/sources/Scientific-Python-Sundnes.lingua#r_1V7KWH76GSD7XMA9TQVSTEQSWB) chapters 3–4 to write a loop and a separate comprehension expressing the result. Specify expectations before executing either. Test empty, singleton, all-matching, no-matching and repeated-identical-row inputs. Reuse [Give every input a meaning and a checkable schema](../../inquiry/reproducible-computation/data/Meaning-and-schema.lingua#r_6R0FJMACN4F6T6R0ETDCQHF6NH) to explain why missing rows, missing fields, an empty valid result and a payload equal to zero have different meanings.

